NICE specifies tasks, knowledge statements, and skill statements per work role. DigComp specifies competence areas designed for citizen self-assessment. Different questions, different shapes.
See the full cross-framework analysis.

Reproduce this
library(cybedtools)
library(dplyr)

rdf &lt;- load_combined_ntriples_graph()
The ranking is read straight out of the prep script, so this block cannot drift away from what actually ran:

nice_role_counts &lt;- role_framework_bindings(rdf) |&gt;
  filter(grepl("^NICE", framework_name)) |&gt;
  inner_join(
    role_element_bindings(rdf) |&gt; count(role, name = "element_count"),
    by = "role"
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(element_count)) |&gt;
  select(role_name, element_count)

role_framework_bindings() is the right binding here rather than organizing_unit_framework_bindings(). Since the v2.2.0 re-ingest, NICE contributes 11 competency areas as organizing units alongside its work roles, and a unit-level pull would fold them into this ranking.
The full prep script is concordance/_data-prep-top-work-roles.R in the repository.
